(5085884, 741547, 68947, '<td valign="top">This has a really deep meaning and love the grunge of the doll.  I think that perhaps, what would have made this a better shot might have been had the doll been a little further back/more fully exposed in the shot.  As it stands right now, the stack in the background is blurred (I see where you were going with this though) and between the two objects, there''s not quite enough emphasis on the doll (perhaps, more exposure on the doll???) to contrast the blurred stack in the background.  It appears as though both objects are equal and one doesn''t catch your eye more than the other so it leaves it with the two objects competing and therefore, a flatter image.</td>', 'This has a really deep meaning and love the grunge of the doll.  I think that perhaps, what would have made this a better shot might have been had the doll been a little further back/more fully exposed in the shot.  As it stands right now, the stack in the background is blurred (I see where you were going with this though) and between the two objects, there''s not quite enough emphasis on the doll (perhaps, more exposure on the doll???) to contrast the blurred stack in the background.  It appears as though both objects are equal and one doesn''t catch your eye more than the other so it leaves it with the two objects competing and therefore, a flatter image.', '2008-11-26 13:09:25.000000', NULL, 1),
(5085889, 741539, 68947, '<td valign="top">Not sure on what to say here because I like this image as the blue area of the flame and the wick of the candle are great!  However, I know how tough it is to capture a great flame but, the blown out part of it just kind of leaves it a bit unflattering.  How to fix that?  I really don''t know because I struggle with flames as well and just don''t try to enter them anymore (until I get the hang of capturing it properly...think there''s a tutorial on that somewhere in here...gotta look that up)</td>', 'Not sure on what to say here because I like this image as the blue area of the flame and the wick of the candle are great!  However, I know how tough it is to capture a great flame but, the blown out part of it just kind of leaves it a bit unflattering.  How to fix that?  I really don''t know because I struggle with flames as well and just don''t try to enter them anymore (until I get the hang of capturing it properly...think there''s a tutorial on that somewhere in here...gotta look that up)', '2008-11-26 13:12:03.000000', NULL, 1),
